\title{Query semantics and internal R contract}
\description{
This specification describes the internal Paged query engine and its Snapshot
reference consumer. All functions below are private to the jdv namespace. The
contract defines row selection, ordering, pagination, validation, and shared
fixtures for later server and browser consumers.
}
\details{
The engine operates on data frames and matrices. Columns are identified by
their \strong{zero-based positions}, so duplicate or unusual column names are
valid. Returned \code{row_index} values are the
\strong{original one-based R row numbers}.
}
\section{Source slicing contract}{


For a data frame, jdv reads each column with \code{x[[j]]} and selects rows by
applying that column's \code{[} method. It never calls table-level \code{[}, including
\verb{[.data.table}, \verb{[.tbl_df}, or \verb{[.sf}. For a matrix it selects a column and
rows with \code{x[rows, j]}. Extraction does not call \code{unclass()} or coerce a
source column before its own subset method runs. Filtering and sorting use
each full column first; only after the original row indices are fixed does
paging slice the requested rows. Classes that need table-level behavior
require a future source adapter.

Paged registration retains the source object and a fixed initial schema. A
request checks a lightweight fingerprint of dimensions, column names, and
column classes. It cannot detect value-only mutation through a shared
reference; callers must register again after changing values. Re-registration
builds a new holder and cache before replacing the current generation.
}

\section{Internal entry points}{
\tabular{ll}{
   Function \tab Result and purpose \cr
   \code{paged_table(x, collation = "C", limits = list())} \tab A \code{jdv_paged_table} environment holding the source, initial schema, probe diagnostics, and bounded caches. \cr
   \code{query_indices(holder, sort = list(), filter = NULL)} \tab Original one-based row numbers after filtering and sorting. \cr
   \code{query_page(holder, offset = 0L, limit = 200L, sort = list(), filter = NULL, cols = NULL)} \tab A page with totals, effective pagination, original row numbers, optional row names, and selected column extraction results. \cr
   \code{query_clear(holder)} \tab Clear all result caches while retaining the same source and initial schema. Returns the holder invisibly. \cr
   \code{query_dispose(holder)} \tab Clear caches and release source/schema references. Subsequent queries fail with \code{view_not_found}. \cr
   \code{snapshot_table(x, collation = "C", limits = list())} \tab Eagerly materialized channels for the independent Snapshot reference consumer. \cr
   \code{snapshot_indices(snapshot, sort = list(), filter = NULL)} \tab Reference row selection and stable ordering from those materialized channels. \cr
}


For example, this internal R request sorts column zero in descending order
and returns the first twenty rows, displaying columns zero and two:

