\code{sort} is an unnamed list of objects containing exactly \code{col} and \code{desc}.
\code{desc} is a required, scalar, nonmissing Boolean. A scalar integer-valued
plain R double is a valid selector, as is an R integer; Boolean, complex,
classed, nonfinite, missing, fractional, or out-of-range selectors are
invalid. Sort columns and page columns must not repeat.

The holder's source, initial schema, and collation belong to one fixed table
version. Rebuild the holder when source, schema, or the owning registry's
generation changes. \code{query_clear()} alone does not change any of them. The
holder itself does not assign table IDs or manage registry generations.
}

\section{Bounded registration and requested computation}{


Registration probes the first 32 rows by default. For a matrix, it slices and
probes the first column once and reuses that homogeneous type/schema result
for all column positions and names. It retains the original matrix rather
than extracting every full column. For a data frame, it probes a finite row
slice of each existing column reference. Probe extraction includes display,
missingness, values, and sort keys; it does not compute full-column filter
text.

Registration does not create automatic row names or a full \code{row_index}
vector. Without a sort or filter, original row order is generated when
requested. Page display extracts actual requested row slices: \code{x[rows, j]}
for a matrix, or a class-preserving slice of a data-frame column. Display is
formatted for that slice, independently of full-column filter text.

A filter or sort retrieves only its referenced full source columns and
required channels. An \code{other} text filter requests the full column's
\code{format()} output at that point. It never substitutes a page's display text.
Custom subsetting must preserve the column class and requested row count.
Atomic S3 classes without a subset method retain their metadata; classes with
row-dependent attributes must supply suitable subsetting behavior.

Three least-recently-used caches have separate entry limits:\tabular{lrl}{
   Cache \tab Default entries \tab Contents \cr
   Source column \tab 32 \tab Successfully retrieved full source columns. \cr
   Channel \tab 32 \tab Successful full-column channel results, keyed by column and channel. \cr
   Index \tab 8 \tab Filtered/sorted row indices, keyed by the validated request. \cr
}


Hits refresh recency, and insertion evicts the least recent entry beyond the
configured count. These are entry limits, not byte or total-memory
guarantees; one column or index can still be large. Every request is
validated before an index-cache hit can be reused.

Failed channel and index computations are not cached. A successfully fetched
source column can remain cached after its channel computation fails. A
repaired method can therefore succeed on a subsequent channel request without
clearing the holder, provided its fixed initial schema permits that
operation. A repair that changes a capability rejected by the initial probe
requires rebuilding the holder. Probe diagnostics remain attached to the
original schema.

jdv's own query code does not change RNG or options. Arbitrary class methods
can have side effects, and a query boundary is not a transaction around them.
}

\section{Validation and default limits}{


Object fields are strict: missing required fields, duplicate fields, unknown
fields, malformed objects, or unknown operators are errors. \code{NULL} filter
means no filtering. JSON objects and arrays correspond to ordinary named and
unnamed R lists respectively; no request string is evaluated as R code.\tabular{lrl}{
   Limit key \tab Default \tab Meaning \cr
   \code{probe_rows} \tab 32 \tab Maximum registration probe rows. \cr
   \code{max_columns} \tab 4096 \tab Maximum registered table columns. \cr
   \code{page_rows} \tab 2000 \tab Maximum returned rows per page; larger requested limits are clamped. \cr
   \code{page_columns} \tab 256 \tab Maximum selected page columns; excess is an error. \cr
   \code{sort_columns} \tab 16 \tab Maximum sort terms. \cr
   \code{ast_depth} \tab 32 \tab Maximum filter depth, counting the root as depth one. \cr
   \code{ast_nodes} \tab 256 \tab Maximum total filter nodes and maximum factor-membership labels. \cr
   \code{text_bytes} \tab 4096 \tab Maximum UTF-8 bytes in each string argument. \cr
   \code{filter_bytes} \tab 65536 \tab Maximum summed UTF-8 bytes in text/date/datetime/decimal/factor-label arguments. \cr
   \code{column_cache} \tab 32 \tab Source-column cache entries. \cr
   \code{channel_cache} \tab 32 \tab Full-column channel cache entries. \cr
   \code{index_cache} \tab 8 \tab Result-index cache entries. \cr
}


Internal overrides are supplied as a named plain list of recognized keys and
positive scalar integers. Their supported range ends at
\code{.Machine$integer.max}; \code{ast_depth} has an additional ceiling of 128.
Operator names are separately bounded to 32 UTF-8 bytes. Factor-label
strings, datetime/date strings, and integer64 decimal strings contribute to
the total filter-string budget as well as their individual argument limits.

\code{offset} and \code{limit} are nonnegative finite scalar integers, with
integer-valued plain doubles accepted. Values above \code{2^53} are rejected.
\code{offset} is zero-based within the filtered/sorted result. \code{limit = 0} and
offsets beyond the result produce an empty page; query validation and
total-result computation still apply. A larger limit is clamped to
\code{page_rows}.

\code{cols = NULL} selects every column, preserving source order. It does not
bypass \code{page_columns}: a table with more than 256 columns needs an explicit
smaller selection or an appropriate internal limit override. Excess columns
are never silently omitted. An empty explicit selection returns no data
columns while still returning row indices and totals. Selected columns follow
selector order.
}

\section{Filter AST}{


Filter objects use these exact fields:\tabular{lll}{
   Operator \tab Required fields \tab Supported columns \cr
   \code{and}, \code{or} \tab \code{op}, \code{args} (array of child objects) \tab Any valid children. \cr
   \code{not} \tab \code{op}, \code{arg} (one child object) \tab Any valid child. \cr
   \code{is_na} \tab \code{op}, \code{col} \tab Any column with a valid missingness channel. \cr
   \code{contains}, \code{starts_with}, \code{ends_with}, \code{text_equals} \tab \code{op}, \code{col}, \code{value} \tab String, factor, filterable other. \cr
   \code{eq}, \code{ne}, \code{lt}, \code{le}, \code{gt}, \code{ge} \tab \code{op}, \code{col}, \code{value} \tab Integer, number, date, datetime; integer64 supports only \code{eq}. \cr
   \code{between} \tab \code{op}, \code{col}, \code{lower}, \code{upper} \tab Integer, number, date, datetime, integer64. \cr
   \code{is_true}, \code{is_false} \tab \code{op}, \code{col} \tab Boolean. \cr
   \verb{in} \tab \code{op}, \code{col}, \code{values} (array of labels) \tab Factor. \cr
}


Example:

\if{html}{\out{<div class="sourceCode json">}}\preformatted{\{
  "op": "and",
  "args": [
    \{"op": "contains", "col": 2, "value": "ber"\},
    \{"op": "between", "col": 0, "lower": 1, "upper": 3\},
    \{"op": "not", "arg": \{"op": "is_na", "col": 4\}\}
  ]
\}
}\if{html}{\out{</div>}}

Logical operators combine Boolean results elementwise. Empty \code{and} is true
for every row; empty \code{or} is false. \code{not} performs ordinary Boolean
inversion. Missing source values never match a comparison, membership,
Boolean, or text leaf. Consequently, negating such a false leaf includes
missing rows: use an explicit \verb{not is_na} condition when that inclusion is
unwanted.

\code{is_na} uses the source column's missingness mask, including NaN. It is
allowed even when \code{filterable} is false, provided extraction yields a valid
mask. Missing meaning never comes from display labels such as the literal
string \code{"NA"}.
}

\section{Text}{


Text comparison folds \strong{ASCII A-Z only} to a-z with \code{chartr()} after UTF-8
conversion. Non-ASCII characters retain their case: \code{Ä} and \code{ä} remain
distinct. Matching uses fixed literal strings, not regular expressions.
Brackets, dots, and asterisks have no pattern meaning. An empty needle
matches every nonmissing text for contains/prefix/suffix operations;
\code{text_equals} with an empty value matches only an empty source string.

Text sources are:
\itemize{
\item String: the original, untruncated character value.
\item Factor: its label.
\item Other: \code{format()} over the complete column, computed and cached on request.
}

Number, integer, Boolean, date, datetime, and integer64 columns do not
support text operators. List, matrix, and packed data-frame columns have
previews but no text-filter capability. Page display truncation or formatting
does not alter the filter source. Factor \verb{in} is exact, case-sensitive label
membership and does not use text folding; duplicate labels are harmless and
an empty label array matches no rows.
}

\section{Numeric values and infinity}{


Numeric leaves compare source values without display rounding or an equality
tolerance. Extremely small distinct finite values remain distinct. \code{between}
includes both endpoints and rejects reversed bounds.

Queries accept a finite scalar plain R integer/double or one of these
objects:

\if{html}{\out{<div class="sourceCode json">}}\preformatted{\{"special": "pos_inf"\}
}\if{html}{\out{</div>}}

\if{html}{\out{<div class="sourceCode json">}}\preformatted{\{"special": "neg_inf"\}
}\if{html}{\out{</div>}}

These are the only nonfinite query tokens. Missing/null, NaN, \code{"nan"}, and
\code{"na"} queries are rejected. Direct nonfinite R numeric arguments are also
rejected; use the explicit token. Source -Inf sorts and compares below every
finite value, and source Inf above every finite value. NA and NaN source rows
remain false at comparison leaves. Materialized number channels recover
infinities from \code{special}; null values alone do not distinguish all cases.
}

\section{Date and datetime}{


Date operands are strings of exactly \code{YYYY-MM-DD}. Datetime operands require
\code{YYYY-MM-DDTHH:MM:SS}, optionally followed by decimal fractional seconds and
either \code{Z} or an explicit \code{+HH:MM}/\code{-HH:MM} offset. With no offset they use
the column's timezone, or the local system timezone when the column has no
explicit one. Explicit offsets identify instants independently of that local
wall time.

Invalid calendar dates are rejected. Hours must be 00-23, minutes/seconds
00-59; offset hours/minutes must be within 00-23/00-59. Whitespace, alternate
layouts, and trailing content are invalid. A valid-looking datetime that R
cannot represent in the timezone is rejected. Daylight-saving gaps and
ambiguities otherwise follow R's platform/timezone parser; these inputs do
not have a portable normalization guarantee. Fractional seconds use R's
representable timestamp precision. Comparisons use canonical day/second
values, independently of decorated class display methods.
}

\section{Integer64}{


Integer64 operands must be canonical decimal strings matching \code{0} or
\verb{-?[1-9][0-9]*}, in the finite range
\verb{[-9223372036854775807, 9223372036854775807]}. Numeric operands, plus signs,
leading zeros, \code{-0}, whitespace, exponent notation, missing values, overflow,
and the reserved NA value \code{-9223372036854775808} are invalid.

\code{eq} and inclusive \code{between} compare the original integer64 bits in C,
retaining exact distinctions beyond \code{2^53}. Other numeric operators are
unsupported for integer64. Reversed bounds are invalid, and missing rows
compare false. A custom integer64 subclass may decorate display through its
format method; values, ordering, and filters remain exact C operations.
}

\section{Stable multi-column sort}{


Terms are applied in array order, with independent ascending/descending
flags. If a pair of keys is equal, including two missing keys, comparison
continues to the next term. If every term ties, original row number breaks
the tie in ascending order. NA and NaN are equivalent missing keys and come
last in both directions. The source missingness mask overrides any finite key
produced for a missing source value; missing keys themselves also sort last.\tabular{ll}{
   Column kind \tab Comparison keys \cr
   Plain Boolean/integer/number \tab Numeric values, including restored infinities. \cr
   Plain string, collation \code{C} \tab UTF-8 byte/radix order. Paged uses the original strings; Snapshot uses dense numeric ranks. \cr
   String, collation \code{locale} \tab R \code{xtfrm()} keys under the system locale. \cr
   Factor/ordered factor \tab Factor level codes, regardless of alphabetical label order. \cr
   Date/POSIXct/POSIXlt \tab Canonical days/seconds. \cr
   Integer64 \tab C-generated dense ranks with equal keys for equal values. \cr
   Other sortable class \tab Its \code{xtfrm()} keys; nonmissing nonfinite keys or keys outside \verb{[-2^53, 2^53]} become dense ranks. \cr
}


Paged passes the selected keys to one radix \code{order()} call with a per-term
\code{decreasing} vector and missing values last. It does not use an order
permutation as a sort key, because that would lose ties. Character C sorting
avoids an unnecessary full-column rank computation in Paged mode.

Snapshot consumers must compare supplied numeric keys rather than
JavaScript's raw UTF-16 string order or an independent browser locale
collation. In the \code{utf8-byte-order} fixture, U+E000 sorts before U+1F600 (the
grinning-face emoji) under C order, whereas raw JavaScript string comparison
can reverse them. Locale keys likewise preserve the originating R locale's
comparison rather than requesting a new locale sort in the consumer.
}

\section{Page results and channel errors}{


The page result has these fields:\tabular{ll}{
   Field \tab Meaning \cr
   \code{total} \tab Number of rows matching the filter. \cr
   \code{total_unfiltered} \tab Original row count. \cr
   \code{offset} \tab Validated requested offset. \cr
   \code{limit} \tab Effective row limit after clamping. \cr
   \code{data$row_index} \tab Original one-based row numbers for this page. \cr
   \code{data$row_names} \tab Selected explicit row-name strings, or \code{NULL} for automatic/absent row names. \cr
   \code{data$columns} \tab Selected columns in requested order; each is an extraction result containing \code{schema}, \code{data}, and \code{errors}. \cr
}


Page column data requests display, missingness, and values only; it does not
contain sort keys or full-column filter text. Display is always character
text, with missing display results normalized to \code{"NA"}. A display failure
can yield \verb{<class>} fallback text and column diagnostics. Missingness and
semantic values remain independent of display. For a number column, \code{special}
distinguishes NA, NaN, Inf, and -Inf when present. Columns without a plain
values representation can return display and missingness without values.

Page extraction preserves per-channel downgrade diagnostics. Sort/filter
requests that require an unavailable full-column channel instead raise a
typed query error. Their condition's \code{details} can contain the extraction's
\code{schema}, \code{data}, and \code{errors}; no failed channel result enters the cache.
The initial holder schema is not overwritten by a later extraction result.

Snapshot wire encoding may omit \code{display} for string, factor, and integer64
columns only when every nonmissing display value equals its semantic value
and every missing display value is the standard string \code{"NA"}. The
missingness mask then reconstructs the omitted missing labels. A
class-specific formatter that uses another missing label keeps its \code{display}
channel, including when all rows are missing. Zero-row columns use the same
equality checks, with empty comparisons treated as equal.

Conditions inherit from \code{jdv_query_error}, \code{error}, and \code{condition}, and
expose \code{code}, \code{message}, and optional \code{details}:\tabular{ll}{
   Code \tab Typical cause \cr
   \code{invalid_request} \tab Malformed objects, selectors, directions, operands, or reversed bounds. \cr
   \code{unsupported} \tab A valid request uses a column capability/type/operator combination that is unavailable. \cr
   \code{limit_exceeded} \tab Registration, page width, sort width, AST, or string budget is exceeded. \cr
   \code{schema_changed} \tab A column's full shape/class differs from its probe, subsetting changes class/size, or a required channel returns an invalid/missing result. \cr
   \code{r_error} \tab Source access, class methods, or another requested R operation fails. \cr
   \code{view_not_found} \tab The holder has been disposed. \cr
}
}

\section{Independent Snapshot reference and shared fixtures}{


\code{snapshot_table()} materializes the column channels, including full filter
text. Its reference consumer evaluates filters one row at a time and
implements lexicographic scalar comparison with a stable merge sort. It does
not call the Paged \code{order()} or vectorized filter evaluator. Request
validation, operand parsing, and ASCII folding are shared so accepted
requests have one meaning. Integer64 reference filtering compares canonical
decimal strings by sign, digit count, and digits rather than converting them
to double.

For supported inputs with valid required channels, Paged and Snapshot must
return identical original row indices for every sort/filter request. Display
strings may differ because R format methods can inspect the whole vector or
the requested page when choosing a format.

The version 1 query fixture ships with the package. Its \code{fold} entries record
ASCII folding. Each \code{cases} entry describes typed source columns and
\code{queries} with expected original one-based row indices. Number \code{special}
entries reconstruct nonfinite source values; JSON null means missing data.
Factor columns include levels and orderedness; datetime columns include
timezone metadata.

\code{example-other} uses a synthetic \code{query_record} class factory: its fixture
\code{values} array supplies the numeric constructor input, with JSON null
becoming R missing data. Its \code{format()} method returns
\code{paste0("value:", as.numeric(x))}, \code{xtfrm()} returns \code{as.numeric(x)}, and
missingness follows \code{is.na(x)}. The class's runtime \code{values} channel is
absent (\code{NULL}). The JSON fixture's numeric factory input is therefore not an
\code{other} column's wire values or a numeric-filter capability.

